# What must never enter a build context.
#
# There was no .dockerignore at all until now, and its absence broke the
# production image in two separate ways.
#
# 1. CORRECTNESS. `COPY packages/ ./packages/` copied the HOST's node_modules
#    straight over the ones pnpm had just installed. pnpm's node_modules are a
#    tree of symlinks into the store, so on a Windows or macOS host those links
#    point at host paths that do not exist inside a Linux container: the image
#    ended up with `packages/api-contracts/node_modules/typescript` pointing at
#    `F:/...`, and the contracts build died with MODULE_NOT_FOUND.
#
# 2. SECRECY. The same COPY would have baked `apps/backend/.env` into an image
#    layer, where it stays readable to anyone who can pull the image even if a
#    later layer deletes it.
#
# Everything the image needs is either committed source or built inside the
# container, so excluding all of the below costs nothing.

# Dependencies — always reinstalled in the image
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output — always rebuilt in the image
dist
**/dist
.next
**/.next
.expo
**/.expo
.turbo
**/.turbo

# Secrets. `.env.example` is committed documentation and stays.
.env
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# History, tooling and local scratch
.git
.gitignore
.github
.claude
**/*.log
coverage
**/coverage
temp_deploy
test_standalone
deploy-output
tmp
__pycache__
